+# ajv vs zod: validating draft 2020-12 JSON Schema documents
+
+**Short answer:** Use **ajv**. It validates data against draft 2020-12 JSON Schema documents. **zod** does not. It is a TypeScript schema library with its own API. It can export its schemas *to* JSON Schema, but it is not a JSON Schema validator.
+
+## Sources: unverified
+
+- This repo has no `node_modules` and no `package.json`, so I had no installed source to cite.
+- The fetches of the docs sites (ajv.js.org, zod.dev) were blocked in this session.
+- The facts below come from my knowledge of the projects' docs and source. Check them against the links before relying on them, especially the version-specific details.
+
+## ajv: yes, but you have to choose the draft-2020-12 class
+
+- **Docs:** https://ajv.js.org/json-schema.html (section "draft 2020-12") and https://ajv.js.org/guide/schema-language.html
+- **Usage:** the default export (`require("ajv")`) targets **draft-07**. For 2020-12 you import a separate class:
+  ```js
+  const Ajv2020 = require("ajv/dist/2020") // or: import Ajv2020 from "ajv/dist/2020"
+  const ajv = new Ajv2020()
+  const validate = ajv.compile(schema) // schema with "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema"
+  ```
+- **Where to check in source** (once installed): `node_modules/ajv/dist/2020.js` (and `lib/2020.ts` in the repo). The meta-schemas are under `node_modules/ajv/dist/refs/json-schema-2020-12/`. The 2020-12 vocabularies include `prefixItems`, `$dynamicRef`/`$dynamicAnchor`, and `unevaluatedProperties`/`unevaluatedItems`.
+- **Caveats:**
+  - One Ajv instance supports only one draft family. You can't mix draft-07 and 2020-12 schemas in the same instance.
+  - Validation of `format` keywords needs the separate `ajv-formats` package.
+
+## zod: no, it doesn't validate JSON Schema documents
+
+- **Docs:** https://zod.dev and https://zod.dev/json-schema
+- Zod schemas are built in code (`z.object({...})`), and `.parse()`/`.safeParse()` validate data against *those* schemas. Zod has no API for compiling an arbitrary JSON Schema document and validating data against it.
+- **Zod 4 adds `z.toJSONSchema(schema)`.** It *emits* JSON Schema, with draft 2020-12 as the default target. This goes one way only: Zod to JSON Schema. It's useful for publishing schemas, not for consuming them.
+- Any JSON Schema → Zod conversion (third-party packages like `json-schema-to-zod`, or newer experimental helpers in Zod) is a lossy code-generation step. It is not a conformant 2020-12 validator. Keywords like `$dynamicRef` and `unevaluated*` aren't faithfully supported.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+If the input is **JSON Schema 2020-12 documents**, use `ajv` (with `ajv/dist/2020`) plus `ajv-formats`.
+
+Choose zod only if you want to write schemas in TypeScript and get type inference. In that case JSON Schema is an output, not an input.
